  "title": "binary search, bounds, rotated arrays, and monotone answers",
  "status": "source-backed-scope-complete",
  "concepts": [
    "Binary search repeatedly halves a candidate interval and requires an ordering or monotonicity property that justifies discarding half.",
    "Exact search, lower bound, and upper bound answer different questions when values may be duplicated.",
    "A clear interval convention and loop invariant determine endpoint initialization, midpoint choice, updates, and termination.",
    "Half-open intervals simplify empty ranges and sentinel results; closed intervals are also correct when every endpoint rule is consistent.",
    "Binary search on an answer searches a monotone feasibility predicate rather than looking for a value in an input array.",
    "Rotated sorted arrays retain a sorted half that can guide each elimination step when values are distinct.",
    "A row-major matrix whose rows are ordered can be treated as a virtual one-dimensional sorted array using index conversion.",
    "Search bounds must be proven safe, midpoint arithmetic must avoid overflow, and impossible cases need explicit handling.",
    "Binary search is inappropriate when the predicate is not monotone or the data structure cannot provide efficient random access."
  ],
